# Build Your Own LifeOS — the chapter book

**What you are building:** your own personal Life OS, from an empty folder to a system someone
else could install. Not notes about one. A working thing you use.

**How it works.** One chapter = one hour = one working piece. You direct, I write the code, you
run a command that proves it works. If the check fails, the chapter isn't done.

**Where it lives:** `C:\Users\Boris\Dell\stuff\Docs\Learning\AI\PAI\build\myos\`
Your real `~/.claude` is production for six client projects. We never touch it.

**Every chapter has the same six parts:**

| Part | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Why** | What this piece is for, in plain language, before any command |
| **Where you are** | What exists on disk right now, at the start of this hour |
| **Where you'll be** | What exists at the end |
| **Sources** | Only when needed, and always with the exact range to read. `✅ verified` means I opened the page and read its real headings |
| **Do** | Numbered. The prompt to send me, then the command you run yourself |
| **Proof / Learned** | The command whose output decides if the chapter passed, and what it bought you |

**On sources.** Most chapters need none: you learn by doing and I explain as we go. Where a
source genuinely helps, you get the section to read and what to skip. **No YouTube links appear
in this book.** I cannot fetch transcripts or chapter markers from this machine, so I cannot
verify a single timestamp, and a guessed one sends you to the wrong minute.

---

# The whole arc

| Part | Chapters | Hours | You end up with |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1. A system that knows you** | 1–6 | 6 | A session that knows who you are and what you're working toward |
| **2. A system that acts** | 7–13 | 7 | Skills that fire on plain sentences, one of them running real code |
| **3. A system that enforces** | 14–20 | 7 | Rules that hold whether or not the model cooperates |
| **4. A system that remembers** | 21–26 | 6 | Facts learned Monday, used Friday, without you repeating them |
| **5. A system that verifies** | 27–32 | 6 | Claims that cannot close without evidence |
| **6. A system you can see** | 33–37 | 5 | A dashboard, a voice, a health check |
| **7. A system you can give away** | 38–42 | 5 | An installer, an update path, a README |
| | **42** | **42 h** | |

At 5 hours a week that's 8 weeks. At 10, it's 4.

