# PART 1 — A SYSTEM THAT KNOWS YOU

---

## Chapter 1 — Make an empty folder into a system that loads

**Why.** Every AI system in this book, LifeOS included, starts as one markdown file that the
assistant reads before you type anything. Get that file loading and everything else has
somewhere to attach. Get it wrong and every later chapter fails quietly, because your rules
will be sitting in a file nobody reads.

**Where you are.** Nothing exists. `build/myos/` is not there yet.

**Where you'll be.** A folder that, when you start a session inside it, loads your file. You
will have *seen* it load, not assumed it.

**Sources.** None. This chapter is 100% doing.

### Do

**1. Make the folder.** In your Ubuntu terminal:
```bash
mkdir -p /mnt/c/Users/Boris/Dell/stuff/Docs/Learning/AI/PAI/build/myos
cd /mnt/c/Users/Boris/Dell/stuff/Docs/Learning/AI/PAI/build/myos
```

**2. Start a session in it.** Type `claude` and press enter. You are now in a session whose
working folder is `myos`, not `~/.claude`. This matters: files are found relative to where you
started.

**3. Send me this prompt, exactly:**

> Create `CLAUDE.md` in this folder. It should be a routing table, not a rulebook: a title, one
> sentence saying this is Boris's personal Life OS, and a short list of where things will live
> (`USER/` for identity, `skills/` for capabilities, `hooks/` for enforcement). Keep it under 30
> lines. Do not add rules yet.

**4. Read what I wrote** before doing anything else. `cat CLAUDE.md`. If you don't understand a
line, ask. A file you can't read is a file you can't debug in chapter 20.

### Proof

Still in that session, type:
```
/context
```
Look for a **Memory files** section listing your `CLAUDE.md`. If it isn't there, the file is in
the wrong place or the session was started from the wrong folder.

Second proof, the one that actually matters. **Quit the session** (`Ctrl-D`), start a fresh one
in the same folder, and ask:

> What is this project and where do skills go in it?

It should answer from your file without you pasting anything. That is the whole point: the file
is loaded before you speak.

### Learned

A context file is not configuration, it's a message delivered to the assistant before your
first word. **Where you use it:** every repo you own. A `CLAUDE.md` in `9T_erp` is why I'd stop
asking you where the migrations live.

---

## Chapter 2 — Teach it who you are

**Why.** Right now your system knows about a *project*. It doesn't know about *you*. Identity
is what turns generic advice into advice for a Georgian ERP builder with eight live projects.
We put it in a separate file rather than in `CLAUDE.md`, because identity changes on a different
clock than project layout, and because in Part 7 identity is the part that must survive updates.

> **"But I'm building this for other people. Why would they want my details?"**
> They wouldn't, and they never get them. What ships is the **empty slot and the interview that
> fills it**, never your answers. Check the real thing: `skills/LifeOS/install/USER/PRINCIPAL/PRINCIPAL_IDENTITY.md`
> in the public LifeOS release reads `Name: User`, `Location: (interview)`, and **no file in the
> shipped USER template contains the word "Boris" or "Daniel"**. Daniel built his own identity
> file, then released the shape of it.
> You are building three separate things in this chapter, and only the first two ship:
> **(1)** the `@`-import line in `CLAUDE.md`, which is the mechanism · **(2)** a template with
> the right questions in it · **(3)** *your* answers, which stay in the USER zone and are never
> distributed. Chapter 5 is where that boundary gets enforced in code. And you are also the
> first user: a slot that doesn't work for you works for nobody.

**Where you are.** `build/myos/CLAUDE.md` exists and loads.

**Where you'll be.** A second file, `USER/IDENTITY.md`, pulled into every session automatically
by a one-line reference from `CLAUDE.md`.

**Sources.**
📄 <https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory> — ✅ **verified.** Read the section
**"### Import additional files"** only, about 400 words. Skip everything above and below it.
The one thing to notice: an imported file loads at launch, so it costs you context on every
single session whether you use it or not. That's why identity is short and reference material
isn't imported.

### Do

**1. Send me this:**

> Create `USER/IDENTITY.md`. Cover: who I am professionally, where I am, my eight active
> projects with one line each, how I like to work, and what I'm not. Ask me questions where you
> don't know the answer instead of inventing it. Then add the `@`-import line to `CLAUDE.md`.

**2. Answer my questions honestly.** This is the chapter's real work. Vague input here produces
a system that gives vague advice for the next 40 chapters.

**3. Check the import line yourself:**
```bash
grep '@' CLAUDE.md
```
You should see something like `@USER/IDENTITY.md`. One line. That line is the entire mechanism.

### Proof

New session in the folder. Ask three things you never explained in it:

> Which of my projects uses Flyway? What timezone am I in? What do I mean when I say "the ERP"?

Three correct answers, zero prompting, is a pass. One wrong answer means the file is missing a
fact, which is information, not failure.

### Learned

Identity is a file, and `@` is how a file becomes context. **Where you use it:** the first hour
of any client engagement. Their people, their systems, their vocabulary, in one file that every
session reads.

---

## Chapter 3 — Write your TELOS: what you're actually trying to do

**Why.** Identity says who you are. TELOS says where you're going: mission, goals, the problems
you're solving, the strategies you've chosen. It exists so the system can judge a suggestion.
Without it, "should I build X?" gets a generic answer. With it, the answer is measured against
your actual net-worth-by-40 goal and your eight-projects-is-too-many problem. **This is the file
that makes it a Life OS instead of a config folder.**

**Where you are.** The system knows who you are.

**Where you'll be.** It knows what you want, and can say whether a given piece of work serves it.

**Sources.**
📄 `~/.claude/LIFEOS/USER/TELOS/TELOS.md` on your own machine — the one you filled in with
`/Interview`. **Whole file, it's short.** Read it as a shape to copy, not to copy verbatim.
📄 `~/.claude/skills/Interview/SKILL.md` — ✅ **verified.** Read **"## Quick Reference"** and
**"## Gotchas"** only, six bullets. Skip the workflow-routing sections.

### Do

**1. Send me this:**

> write `USER/TELOS.md` based on knowledge you have about me. Sections: Mission, Goals with dates, Problems I'm
> solving, Strategies I've committed to, and Challenges. Don't write "grow the business" as a goal.

**2. Expect this to take most of the hour.** It is the only chapter where thinking outweighs
typing. Being pushed on a woolly goal is the point, not friction.

**3. Import it:** add `@USER/TELOS.md` to `CLAUDE.md`, same as chapter 2.

### Proof

New session. Ask something with no right answer:

> I have a free Saturday. Should I work on the geo-lander redesign or the 9T cash-flow forecast?

A pass is an answer that **cites your TELOS** — naming a goal or a problem by its own words. A
fail is a balanced list of pros and cons, which is what a system with no TELOS produces.

### Learned

Intent, written down once, applied to every future question. **Where you use it:** this is the
thing clients cannot buy anywhere else. Any tool can write code; a system that knows what the
business is *for* can tell them which code not to write.

---

## Chapter 4 — Split the constitution from the routing table

**Why.** `CLAUDE.md` is read as a message: helpful, but the assistant can weigh it against other
things and occasionally lose. A **system prompt** sits at a higher level. LifeOS puts its
non-negotiables there and keeps `CLAUDE.md` as a map of where things live. That split is why on
your machine plain `claude` and `lifeos` behave differently. You're going to build the same
split, because from chapter 16 you'll have rules that must not be negotiable.

**Where you are.** One file holding everything, loaded as context.

**Where you'll be.** Two files and a launcher command, `myos`, that loads both.

**Sources.**
📄 your own `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` — **whole thing**, 104 lines. Notice it contains almost no
rules, only pointers. That restraint is the lesson.
📄 `~/.claude/LIFEOS/LIFEOS_SYSTEM_PROMPT.md` — read **"## Output Format"** and
**"## Verification"** only. Skip the rest; it's reference you'll return to in Part 5.

### Do

**1. Send me this:**

> Create `SYSTEM_PROMPT.md` in this folder with three or four rules I actually want enforced,
> such as never asserting something without checking it first. Then write a `myos` shell alias
> that launches Claude with `--append-system-prompt-file` pointing at it, and show me the exact
> line to add to `~/.bashrc`.

**2. Add the alias yourself** and reload:
```bash
source ~/.bashrc
```
Adding it by hand matters. In chapter 39 you'll write an installer that does this for someone
else, and you should have felt it once.

### Proof

The two-session comparison. Run each and compare:
```bash
claude -p "Quote the first rule in your system prompt, or say you have none."
myos  -p "Quote the first rule in your system prompt, or say you have none."
```
Plain `claude` should have none. `myos` should quote your rule. **Different answers from the
same question is the proof.**

### Learned

There are two levels of instruction, and you now control both. **Where you use it:** when a
client says "it keeps ignoring our rule", the answer is usually that the rule is in the wrong
level. Now you can say so and fix it.

---

## Chapter 5 — Put your rules where updates can't reach them

**Why.** The most expensive mistake in this whole field: a client updates their system and their
customisations vanish. LifeOS solves it by sorting every file into four zones, where SYSTEM ships
and gets overwritten and USER is never touched. You are going to build that boundary now, while
your system is six files, because retro-fitting it later is painful. **This is the most
commercially valuable hour in the book.**

**Where you are.** Everything sits in one flat folder, all equally overwritable.

**Where you'll be.** A tree where you can point at any file and say which zone it's in, and a
test proving the USER zone survives a system overwrite.

**Sources.**
📄 `~/.claude/LIFEOS/DOCUMENTATION/SystemUserBoundary.md` — ✅ **verified.** Read from
**"## The four zones"** to the end of **"## The four allowed access patterns"**, roughly 40% of
the file. Skip the "Why this document exists" preamble entirely.

### Do

**1. Send me this:**

> Restructure `myos` into the four zones from `SystemUserBoundary.md`. Identity, TELOS and my
> rules go in USER. `CLAUDE.md`, `SYSTEM_PROMPT.md` and anything I'd ship go in SYSTEM. Write
> `ZONES.md` listing every current file and its zone, and explain any file that was hard to
> classify.

**2. Read `ZONES.md` and argue with at least one row.** The hard cases are where the
understanding is. `CLAUDE.md` is SYSTEM but it imports USER files: why is that allowed, and what
would break if it weren't?

### Proof

Simulate an update and check nothing personal died:
```bash
cp -r USER /tmp/myos-user-before
echo "# overwritten by a fake update" > CLAUDE.md
diff -r USER /tmp/myos-user-before && echo "USER ZONE SURVIVED"
```
`USER ZONE SURVIVED` is the pass. Then restore your `CLAUDE.md` from git or ask me to rewrite it.

### Learned

The line between what you ship and what belongs to the user. **Where you use it:** every client
install, forever. It's the difference between an upgrade that keeps their data and one that eats
it, and it is the reason your own LifeOS update last week didn't destroy ten months of memory.

---

## Chapter 6 — Checkpoint: the cold-start test

**Why.** Five chapters of building, and no independent evidence any of it works together. A
checkpoint chapter builds nothing new. It tries to break what you have. Every part in this book
ends with one, because a system that has never been tested cold is a system you don't yet know.

**Where you are.** Six or seven files across two zones, a launcher, an identity, a TELOS.

**Where you'll be.** Either a green Part 1, or a specific list of what's broken. Both are wins.
Only "I think it works" is a loss.

**Sources.** None. This is an exam.

### Do

**1. Close everything.** Every session, every terminal. This test is worthless if something is
warm.

**2. Fresh terminal, `cd` to `myos`, run `myos`.** Ask these five, one at a time, and copy each
answer into `build/notes/06-coldstart.md`:

| # | Question | Passes if |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Who am I and what do I do? | Correct, specific, from `IDENTITY.md` |
| 2 | What are my three most active projects? | Names the right three |
| 3 | What's my single most important goal, and by when? | Cites TELOS with the date |
| 4 | What rule must you never break with me? | Quotes a rule from `SYSTEM_PROMPT.md` |
| 5 | Where do skills live in this project? | Correct path from `CLAUDE.md` |

**3. Then try to break it.** Ask something the system should refuse or push back on, based on
the rules you wrote in chapter 4. If it complies where it should resist, that's chapter 16's
problem, and worth writing down now.

**4. Send me this:**

> Here are my five answers and the break attempt. Grade each pass or fail, and for every fail
> tell me which file is at fault and why.

### Proof

5/5, with a written note on the break attempt. Anything less: fix it before Part 2. Skills built
on a broken foundation just hide the crack.

### Learned

That the pieces work as a system, not just individually. **Where you use it:** this exact test
is what you'll run on a client's machine before saying it's ready, and what you'll put in the
README in chapter 41 so they can run it themselves.

